consiSt("sd",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"South Dakota Supreme Court","d":"The state’s highest court and its only appellate court. Hears appeals from the circuit courts directly (there is no intermediate court of appeals), has original jurisdiction over certain proceedings, and administers the Unified Judicial System, including bar admission and lawyer discipline. Civil appellate procedure is codified at SDCL ch. 15-26A."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts (seven judicial circuits)","d":"The general-jurisdiction trial courts for civil and criminal cases, organized into seven judicial circuits, each with a presiding judge. Civil procedure is codified at SDCL ch. 15-6. On October 2, 2026, the State Court Administrator’s Office proposed a rule merging the Fourth and Seventh Judicial Circuits into one administrative circuit; the Supreme Court’s rules hearing was set for November 9, 2026. Check whether the merger was adopted before relying on circuit-specific administration in western South Dakota."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Magistrate Courts","d":"Operate under the supervision of the circuit courts. Handle misdemeanor criminal cases, minor civil actions, small claims, and preliminary hearings in criminal cases. Magistrate judges and nonlaw-trained magistrates are subject to the same affidavit-for-change procedure as circuit judges."},{"l":"OTHER","n":"Tribal courts","d":"Tribal courts of the Native American nations located in South Dakota are separate sovereign court systems, not part of the Unified Judicial System. The UJS website keeps a tribal-courts page. The state bar examination includes an Indian law question."}],"slapp":[{"t":"New anti-SLAPP law: SDCL ch. 15-40 (2026)","d":"South Dakota enacted its first anti-SLAPP statute in 2026 (SL 2026, ch 91), codified as SDCL ch. 15-40, “Expedited Procedure for SLAPPs.” The text follows the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act model. The codified text does not state an effective date. Before relying on it for conduct or suits that predate the Act, confirm when it took effect and whether it applies to pending cases.","c":"SDCL 15-40-1 to 15-40-13; SL 2026, ch 91","v":true},{"t":"Scope: protected public expression","d":"The chapter covers a cause of action based on a person’s: (1) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative or other governmental proceeding; (2) communication on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (3) exercise of free speech, press, assembly, petition or association rights under the U.S. or South Dakota Constitution on a matter of public concern. It does not apply to claims against government units or employees acting in an official capacity, to government enforcement actions against an imminent threat to public health or safety, or to claims against a seller or lessor of goods or services arising from communications about that sale or lease. Artistic, journalistic, literary, musical and political works are not “goods or services.” The chapter is to be broadly construed.","c":"SDCL 15-40-1, 15-40-2, 15-40-13","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: 60-day motion, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling","d":"File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause. Filing stays all other proceedings between the movant and the respondent, including discovery, until the motion is decided and the time to appeal expires. The court may allow limited discovery if specific information is needed and not otherwise available. The court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing (later only for limited discovery or good cause) and rule within 60 days after the hearing. Unrelated motions, public-safety injunctions, fee motions, voluntary dismissals and severance are not stayed.","c":"SDCL 15-40-3, 15-40-4, 15-40-5, 15-40-6, 15-40-7, 15-40-10","v":true},{"t":"Standard: dismissal with prejudice","d":"The court considers the pleadings, motion papers and any evidence admissible on summary judgment. It must dismiss with prejudice if: the movant shows the chapter applies; the respondent fails to show an exclusion; and either the respondent fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the motion or the fee claim, and a voluntary dismissal with prejudice means the movant prevailed.","c":"SDCL 15-40-8, 15-40-9","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory fees and immediate appeal","d":"A prevailing movant must be awarded court costs, reasonable attorney fees and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion. A prevailing respondent gets them only if the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay. A movant may appeal an order denying the motion in whole or in part as of right, within 30 days after entry of the order, and an appeal stays all proceedings between all parties until it ends.","c":"SDCL 15-40-11, 15-40-12, 15-40-4","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: sworn motion, $200, and local counsel who personally participates","d":"A nonresident lawyer licensed in another U.S. jurisdiction may take part in the trial or hearing of a particular case only if a resident South Dakota lawyer, a State Bar member, is actually employed and associated and personally participates in the trial or hearing.\nFile a written sworn motion with the court where admission is sought. It must state: your post-office address; the office address of the associated South Dakota lawyer; good standing in your home state’s bar; no discipline in any state in the past 5 years; no denial of admission to any state or federal court in the past 5 years; familiarity with and agreement to follow the South Dakota rules of conduct; and the sales-tax statement described below.\nInclude a $200 fee (refunded if denied; on grant, $100 goes to the UJS fund and $100 to the State Bar), plus a motion by the resident lawyer saying you are reputable and recommending admission. The judge may examine you on ethical standards and may deny the motion in the judge’s discretion.","c":"SDCL 16-18-2","v":true},{"t":"Sales and use tax license for out-of-state lawyers","d":"The pro hac vice motion must state either that you have applied for, or hold, a South Dakota Sales and Use Tax License from the Department of Revenue, or that you are not in private practice and are appearing for your full-time employer without extra pay. The multijurisdictional-practice rule likewise requires a lawyer providing temporary services in South Dakota to obtain a South Dakota sales tax license and pay the applicable taxes under chapter 10-45. Legal services are taxed here.","c":"SDCL 16-18-2(7); S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(c)(5), (d)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Consequences of pro hac vice admission","d":"Filing the motion submits you to the State Bar Disciplinary Board’s jurisdiction, with venue in the county where the court sits. The admitting judge may revoke admission for professional misconduct, cite you for contempt, and refer you for discipline. Appearing in a contested administrative hearing under SDCL ch. 1-26 follows the same procedure, subject to approval by the circuit court for the county of the hearing or for Hughes County.","c":"SDCL 16-18-2","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice counsel cannot sign attorney subpoenas","d":"Only an attorney of record who is admitted in South Dakota and on the State Bar’s active list may issue a subpoena. The subpoena must be filed with the clerk at the same time it is issued and must carry the required warning text above the signature. In practice the associated South Dakota lawyer must issue subpoenas.","c":"SDCL 15-6-45","v":true},{"t":"Interstate deposition subpoenas: no pro hac vice needed","d":"To take discovery in South Dakota for an out-of-state case, submit the foreign subpoena to the clerk in the county where discovery will happen; the clerk issues a South Dakota subpoena that must conform to SDCL 15-6-45. The request is not an appearance. A lawyer not licensed in South Dakota may litigate enforcement, quash and protective-order disputes over that subpoena without pro hac vice admission.","c":"SDCL 15-6-28.1 to 15-6-28.6, esp. 15-6-28.3","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission (Rule 5.5)","d":"A lawyer admitted and in good standing in another U.S. jurisdiction may provide temporary services in South Dakota that are: done in association with an actively participating South Dakota lawyer; related to a pending or expected proceeding in which the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear; related to arbitration, mediation or other ADR arising from the home practice, where pro hac vice is not required; or otherwise related to the home practice. In every case, the lawyer must obtain a South Dakota sales tax license and pay the tax. In-house counsel may practice for their employer from a South Dakota office.","c":"S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(c), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Permanent admission: exam (not the UBE) or admission without exam","d":"Admission on examination costs $450. The South Dakota exam combines the MEE, MPT, MBE and an Indian law question, with its own passing scores: a 75% average on the written portion, a 133 scaled MBE, and an 85 MPRE. It is not described as a Uniform Bar Examination.\nAdmission without examination costs $650 and requires an ABA-accredited law degree and 3 of the last 5 years in active, lawful practice as a principal occupation, in a state that gives South Dakota lawyers substantially similar admission without exam. There are also limited admissions for legal aid and public defender lawyers, USD law faculty, and military spouses.","c":"SDCL 16-16-12.1, 16-16-13, 16-16-7.1, 16-16-7.6, 16-16-12.3; ch. 16-16 App. (Bar Examiners regs.)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation","d":"Rule 1.2(c) allows a lawyer to limit the scope of representation if the limitation is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. The collected civil rules and e-filing rules contain no specific limited-appearance or ghostwriting procedure, so check local practice and the court’s expectations before entering a limited appearance.","c":"S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(c)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: Odyssey registration required","d":"Lawyers must e-file in all civil cases in circuit and magistrate courts (small claims excepted) and in the Supreme Court through the UJS Odyssey system. Each user must register and designate an e-mail address first, and registration is consent to electronic service. A pro hac vice lawyer should plan for registration, or have local counsel file. Fees are paid at filing, and if a fee waiver is denied the fee is due within 7 days. Paragraphs must be numbered in every e-filed document except briefs.","c":"SDCL 16-21A-2, 16-21A-4(3), 16-21A-7; 15-26C-1","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: South Dakota Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"The Rules are codified as the Appendix to SDCL ch. 16-18, adopted by Supreme Court Rule 03-26 effective January 1, 2004 and amended since (most recently noted: Supreme Court Rule 25-06, effective March 5, 2025, amending Rule 1.8). They follow the ABA Model Rules numbering with the South Dakota differences below. Chapter 16-18 also contains statutory attorney duties (for example, §§ 16-18-13 to 16-18-19) and the attorney’s lien.","c":"SDCL ch. 16-18 App.; SDCL 16-18-13 to 16-18-21","v":true},{"t":"Malpractice-insurance disclosure on letterhead","d":"A lawyer without professional liability insurance of at least $100,000, or whose policy lapses during the representation, must say so on the letterhead in prescribed words (“This lawyer is not covered by professional liability insurance” or the firm equivalent). The disclosure must appear in every written communication with a client. Some trust-account exempt classes, full-time in-house counsel and government lawyers are exempt. There is no ABA Model Rule counterpart.","c":"S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 1.4(c)-(e)","v":true},{"t":"Contingent fees: written notice of expenses","d":"Beyond the ABA requirements, a contingent-fee agreement must clearly tell the client about potential expenses the client will owe whether or not the client prevails.","c":"S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(c)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality exceptions differ from the ABA","d":"South Dakota permits disclosure to prevent the client from committing a criminal act likely to result in imminent death or substantial bodily harm. The ABA standard is reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm, from any cause. South Dakota also permits disclosure to rectify the consequences of a client’s criminal or fraudulent act in which the lawyer’s services were used. It does not have the ABA’s separate exceptions to prevent financial crime or fraud.","c":"S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(b)(1), (4)","v":true},{"t":"No anti-discrimination/harassment misconduct rule","d":"South Dakota Rule 8.4 has paragraphs (a) through (f) only. It does not include ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment or discrimination. Separately, active State Bar members must complete sexual harassment prevention training within two years of admission and every three years after, or be placed on inactive status.","c":"S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4; SDCL 16-18-1.1","v":true},{"t":"Stricter advertising rules","d":"All lawyer communications must be “predominantly informational.” Rule 7.1(c) lists specific forms of misleading communication beyond the ABA rule. Examples: mentioning past success without a disclaimer that it cannot assure future results; unverifiable self-praise; claiming experience that cannot be substantiated; omitting the lawyer’s name and city; and testimonials without a prescribed disclaimer.\nRule 7.2 requires keeping a copy of each advertisement for 2 years and limits fee advertising to listed forms with required disclosures.","c":"S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 7.1(b)-(c), 7.2(c), (g)","v":true},{"t":"Cannabis advice","d":"A lawyer may counsel or assist a client on conduct expressly permitted by South Dakota cannabis laws even if it violates federal law. The lawyer must tell the client that the conduct violates federal law and advise on the federal consequences.","c":"S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(e)","v":true}],"style":"State of South Dakota","home":"https://ujs.sd.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Your action is not commenced until the summons is served","d":"Filing a complaint does not commence an action or stop the statute of limitations. An action is commenced as to each defendant when that defendant (or a codefendant united in interest) is served with the summons. Delivering the summons to the sheriff of the proper county counts only if service or first publication follows within 60 days.","c":"SDCL 15-6-3, 15-2-30, 15-2-31"},{"t":"No notice of entry, no appeal clock, and the notice of appeal can’t be extended","d":"The 30-day appeal time runs only after the judgment is signed, attested, and filed and written notice of entry is given to the adverse party. A notice of entry not filed within 10 days after service is deemed served on its filing date. The Supreme Court cannot enlarge the time to appeal. The notice of appeal is filed in the circuit court, and the clerk must reject it without the docketing statement, proof of service and filing fee.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-6, 15-26A-4(4), 15-26A-92, 15-6-5(d)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions: 10 days, and a 20-day deemed denial","d":"Renewed JMOL and new-trial motions are due 10 days after notice of entry of judgment. If the court does not rule, or extend its time by order, within 20 days after the new-trial motion is served and filed, the motion is deemed denied. The appeal clock then starts on that date with no further notice.","c":"SDCL 15-6-50(b), 15-6-59(b), 15-26A-6"},{"t":"Cross-appeal is a ‘notice of review’ filed in the Supreme Court within 20 days","d":"An appellee seeking relief must file a notice of review with section B of the docketing statement and proof of service with the Supreme Court clerk within 20 days after service of the notice of appeal. Defending the judgment in the brief is not enough.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-22"},{"t":"Order the transcript within 10 days or lose it","d":"The appellant must order the transcript on the Supreme Court form within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, serving the order and filing a copy. Late ordering waives the transcript.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-48, 15-26A-49"},{"t":"Interlocutory review: 10 days from notice of entry","d":"Most pretrial orders are reviewable only by a petition for permission to appeal. File it with the Supreme Court within 10 days after notice of entry of the order, with a 10-page limit and required attachments. The petition does not stay the case.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-3(6), 15-26A-13 to 15-26A-15, 15-26A-19"},{"t":"Short-time periods skip weekends; e-service after midnight slips a day","d":"For periods under 11 days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays are excluded. Three days are added only for mail service, not e-mail or fax. Service by e-mail, fax or Odyssey must be completed by 11:59 p.m. Central time on a non-holiday weekday, or it is deemed made the next weekday. Motions must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, responses 5 days before, and replies 2 days before.","c":"SDCL 15-6-6(a), (d), (e)"},{"t":"Summary judgment runs on a 28/14/7-day clock with numbered fact statements","d":"The motion, brief, statement of undisputed material facts and affidavits must be served at least 28 calendar days before the hearing, the response at least 14, and the reply at least 7. The opponent must answer each numbered fact with record citations. Facts not controverted are admitted.","c":"SDCL 15-6-56(c)"},{"t":"Answer in 30 days, but jury demand in 10","d":"The answer is due 30 days after service of the complaint, not 21 as in federal court. A jury demand must be served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, or the jury is waived.","c":"SDCL 15-6-12(a), 15-6-38(b), (d)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice requires a sales tax license and local counsel at trial","d":"The out-of-state lawyer’s sworn motion must confirm a South Dakota Sales and Use Tax License (or an application for one), unless the lawyer appears for a full-time employer without extra pay. The resident lawyer must personally participate in the trial or hearing, and only South Dakota-admitted active lawyers can issue subpoenas.","c":"SDCL 16-18-2, 15-6-45; S.D. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(c)(5)"},{"t":"Change of judge: ask informally first, and don’t argue anything","d":"The affidavit for change of judge must be preceded by an informal request to the judge. Submitting argument or proof to that judge on any motion waives the right for the rest of the case. The deadline before a jury trial is 15 days.","c":"SDCL 15-12-21.1, 15-12-24, 15-12-27"},{"t":"Costs waived after 30 days; fee motions due in 14","d":"Costs are waived unless a verified application is filed within 30 days after entry of judgment, and objections to costs are due within 10 days after service. A motion for attorney fees is due 14 days after entry of judgment. Appellate attorney fees must be sought by motion with a verified itemized statement before the appeal is submitted.","c":"SDCL 15-6-54(d), 15-26A-87.3"}],"judge":[{"t":"Peremptory change of judge by affidavit: informal request first","d":"South Dakota allows one change of judge per side, without stating a reason, by affidavit. Before filing, the party or lawyer must informally ask the assigned judge or magistrate to step aside, by letter, orally, or on the record. Opposing parties should be told of the request but cannot contest it. If the judge declines, the judge notifies the parties in writing, and the affidavit may then be filed.","c":"SDCL 15-12-21, 15-12-21.1, 15-12-25","v":true},{"t":"Affidavit contents, filing and service","d":"The affidavit must state the case title and that it is made in good faith and not for delay. It must also state that the case is expected to come on for trial before the named judge, and that the party believes it cannot get a fair and impartial trial before that judge. No grounds need be stated. Name only one judge. Parties united in interest must join, and one party’s filing counts for all. File with the clerk of the circuit court in the county where the case is pending, and serve all adverse parties the same day. Failing to serve does not void the affidavit but makes the filer liable for resulting expenses.","c":"SDCL 15-12-23, 15-12-26, 15-12-30, 15-12-31","v":true},{"t":"Deadlines and waiver","d":"Against the judge who would preside in the ordinary course, file:\n• for a noticed motion, at least 2 days before the hearing;\n• for a nonjury trial, at least 5 days before trial;\n• for a jury trial, at least 15 days before trial;\n• against a replacement judge, within 2 days after notice of the replacement’s name.\nLate-retained counsel must file within 5 days after appointment or retention.\nArguing or presenting proof to the judge on any motion or at trial, or failing to appear after notice, waives the right for the rest of the case, including post-judgment proceedings.","c":"SDCL 15-12-24, 15-12-27, 15-12-28, 15-12-29","v":true},{"t":"Who decides and what happens","d":"The presiding judge of the circuit (or the senior judge if the presiding judge is the target) reviews timeliness and waiver and assigns another judge. If every judge in the circuit is disqualified, the Chief Justice assigns one. Orders the disqualified judge already signed stay in effect. Judges must recuse on their own motion when a Canon 3E ground exists, unless the parties waive under Canon 3F. (The Code of Judicial Conduct, Appendix to SDCL ch. 16-2, was not collected.)","c":"SDCL 15-12-22, 15-12-32, 15-12-34, 15-12-35, 15-12-37","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"South Dakota Constitution | Broader than federal: not established here","d":"The South Dakota Constitution has its own free-speech guarantee, and the new anti-SLAPP chapter protects rights “guaranteed by the United States Constitution or South Dakota Constitution.” The constitutional text and the case law were not collected, so no section number is given here. Nothing here establishes that South Dakota protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment.","c":"SDCL 15-40-1(3), 15-40-13; S.D. Const. (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Expedited civil actions for claims of $75,000 or less","d":"A plaintiff whose only relief sought is a money judgment, with all claims by or against any one party totaling $75,000 or less, may elect an expedited civil action by certification; the judgment is then capped at $75,000 (excluding interest and costs), and parties may also stipulate into the track. Each side gets 10 interrogatories, 10 production requests and 10 requests for admission (discrete subparts counted), one deposition of each party plus 2 nonparty depositions, and 1 retained expert. Discovery closes 60 days before trial; each party may file only one summary-judgment motion, due 90 days before trial; trial is set within 1 year of filing with 6 hours per side. Judgments have preclusive effect only for parties and those in privity.","c":"SDCL 15-6-72 to 15-6-76.1","v":true},{"t":"Court reporters tied to insurers are disqualified","d":"No deposition may be taken before a reporter who has a contractual relationship with a person or entity interested in the outcome, including anyone who may ultimately pay, such as an insurer. Reporters may not enter into prohibited reporting agreements.","c":"SDCL 15-6-28(c)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"South Dakota Rules of Civil Appellate Procedure, SDCL ch. 15-26A; Disposition of Appeals, SDCL ch. 15-30 (rehearing, costs, remittitur); Supreme Court Electronic Filing Rules, SDCL ch. 15-26C; Rules of Civil Procedure SDCL 15-6-5(d), 15-6-50, 15-6-54(b), 15-6-58, 15-6-59, 15-6-62; anti-SLAPP appeal, SDCL 15-40-11 (SL 2026, ch 91). Official text from sdlegislature.gov (codified laws shown as effective November 5, 2026). Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in South Dakota: one appellate court, and the clock starts with notice of entry","d":"South Dakota has no intermediate appellate court. Civil appeals from the circuit courts go directly to the South Dakota Supreme Court, which is both the first and the last stop for state-law review.\nThe 30-day appeal clock does not start when the judgment is signed. It starts only after the judgment or order is signed, attested, and filed and written notice of entry has been given to the adverse party. The winning side usually serves notice of entry to start the clock; until it does, the time does not run.\nThe notice of appeal is filed with the clerk of the circuit court, not the Supreme Court, and the clerk must reject it unless it comes with a docketing statement, proof of service, and the filing fee. The Supreme Court cannot extend the time to file or serve a notice of appeal."},"groups":[["What can be appealed",[{"t":"Appeals of right","d":"An appeal lies from: (1) a judgment; (2) an order affecting a substantial right that in effect determines the action and prevents a judgment; (3) an order granting a new trial; (4) a final order affecting a substantial right in special proceedings or on a summary application after judgment; (5) an order granting, refusing, continuing, dissolving, or modifying arrest and bail, claim and delivery, injunction, attachment, garnishment, receivership, or deposit in court; and (7) an order on a motion under SDCL 15-6-11 (sanctions).","c":"SDCL 15-26A-3(1)-(5), (7)"},{"t":"Partial judgments need Rule 54(b) language","d":"In a case with multiple claims or parties, an order deciding fewer than all of them is not final unless the circuit court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment. Without both, the order can be revised and is not appealable as a final judgment.","c":"SDCL 15-6-54(b)"},{"t":"Discretionary appeal of intermediate orders","d":"Any other intermediate order made before trial may be reviewed only by permission of the Supreme Court, granted when the ends of justice are served by deciding the question now. File a petition for permission to appeal with the Supreme Court clerk, with proof of service on all parties and the filing fee, within 10 days after notice of entry of the order. The petition (and any response) is limited to 10 pages, exclusive of the required attachments: a conformed copy of the order, related findings, conclusions or memorandum opinions, and the notice of entry.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-3(6), 15-26A-13, 15-26A-14(7), 15-26A-15"},{"t":"Petition procedure and stays","d":"Any party may respond within 7 days after service of the petition; the petition is decided without argument unless ordered. Filing the petition does not stay the circuit court case; the petitioner must apply for a stay, which may require security. Willful noncompliance or an inaccurate statement in the petition is ground for denial, and a vexatious petition can draw terms. If permission is granted, the order sets the time for the cost bond, briefs and record.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-16, 15-26A-17, 15-26A-18, 15-26A-19, 15-26A-21"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denials are appealable of right","d":"A party whose special motion for expedited relief under the 2026 anti-SLAPP chapter is denied in whole or in part may appeal as a matter of right, no later than 30 days after entry of the order. An appeal from an order ruling on the motion stays all proceedings between all parties until the appeal ends, with limited exceptions.","c":"SDCL 15-40-11, 15-40-4"}]],["Notice of appeal: timing and trigger",[{"t":"30 days after written notice of entry","d":"The appeal must be taken within 30 days after the judgment or order is signed, attested, and filed and written notice of entry has been given to the adverse party. A notice of appeal filed before the signed judgment is attested and filed is treated as filed on the date of attestation and filing.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-6"},{"t":"File the notice of entry promptly","d":"A notice of entry of judgment, with proof of service, must be filed forthwith; if it is not filed within 10 days after service, the time of service is deemed to be the date it is filed. A late-filed notice of entry can therefore move the appeal clock.","c":"SDCL 15-6-5(d)"},{"t":"No extensions of the notice of appeal","d":"The Supreme Court may enlarge most times in chapter 15-26A for good cause, but it may not enlarge the time for filing or serving a notice of appeal.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-92"},{"t":"What the notice must contain and where it is filed","d":"The notice names the appealing parties, designates the judgment or order (or part) appealed from, and is signed by the appellant or counsel. Serve the notice and a docketing statement (on the Supreme Court’s form, with the findings, conclusions and any memorandum decision attached) on counsel for every other party, then file both with the clerk of the trial court before the appeal time expires, with proof of service and the statutory filing fee. The trial clerk must refuse a notice that lacks any of these.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-4(1)-(4)"},{"t":"Only a timely notice is jurisdictional","d":"Failing to take any step other than timely service and filing of the notice of appeal does not void the appeal, but the Supreme Court may act as it deems appropriate, including dismissal.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-4"},{"t":"Amended notices fix clerical errors only","d":"An amended notice may correct clerical errors or omissions. It cannot be used to appeal a later order or judgment unless that later ruling amends the one originally appealed. It does not extend any appellate deadline; times still run from the original notice.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-4.1"},{"t":"Joint and combined appeals","d":"Parties may file a joint notice or join after filing separate timely notices. One notice and one undertaking may cover appeals from several judgments and orders in the same action, subject to each appeal’s time limit. The Chief Justice may consolidate appeals on motion.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-4(6), 15-26A-11"}]],["Post-trial motions that stop the clock",[{"t":"Rule 50(b) and Rule 59 motions terminate the appeal time","d":"A timely motion by any party under SDCL 15-6-59 (new trial) or 15-6-50(b) (renewed judgment as a matter of law) terminates the running of the appeal time for all parties. The full 30 days starts again after the order deciding the motion is signed, attested, and filed and written notice of entry is given.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-6"},{"t":"10-day motion deadlines","d":"A renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law and a motion for new trial must each be served and filed no later than 10 days after notice of entry of judgment.","c":"SDCL 15-6-50(b), 15-6-59(b)"},{"t":"Deemed denial after 20 days","d":"The court must file its order on a new-trial motion within 20 days after service and filing of the motion, unless it files an order within those 20 days extending the time. Otherwise the motion is deemed denied, and the appeal time runs from the date the court’s time to act expired, with no notice of entry needed.","c":"SDCL 15-6-59(b), 15-26A-6"},{"t":"Other motions do not toll","d":"Chapter 15-26A names only motions under 15-6-59 and 15-6-50(b) as terminating the appeal time. A motion under 15-6-60(b) or any other post-judgment motion is not listed; do not rely on it to extend the appeal deadline.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-6"}]],["Cross-appeals: the notice of review",[{"t":"20 days after service of the notice of appeal","d":"An appellee who wants review of a judgment or order that adversely affects it files a notice of review, with section B of the docketing statement and proof of service on all parties, with the clerk of the Supreme Court (not the trial court) within 20 days after service of the notice of appeal. The clerk will not accept it without the docketing statement and proof of service.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-22"},{"t":"Appellee’s brief carries the cross-issues","d":"If a notice of review is filed, the appellee’s brief must include the judgment or order under review and any related findings, conclusions and memorandum decision not already in the appellant’s appendix.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-61"}]],["Bonds and stays",[{"t":"$500 cost bond","d":"Unless exempt, or covered by a supersedeas bond that secures costs, the appellant files a $500 bond for costs (or equivalent security) with the circuit court clerk within the 30-day appeal time. It is waived by filing, within the same time, the written consent of each appellee or an affidavit of indigency. A cash deposit or a written waiver by the protected party may replace an undertaking.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-23, 15-26A-24, 15-26A-41, 15-26A-42"},{"t":"Automatic 30-day stay, then supersedeas","d":"Except on default judgments or as ordered, no execution issues for 30 days after entry of judgment; injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed. After that, an appeal stays enforcement only with a supersedeas bond in the amount and form the circuit court approves. Give at least 24 hours’ notice of an application to fix or approve a bond.","c":"SDCL 15-6-62(a), 15-26A-25, 15-26A-34"},{"t":"Money judgments: bond capped at $25 million","d":"For a money judgment, the bond secures payment of the affirmed amount plus interest from the judgment date. The total bond for all appellants may not exceed $25 million, but the court may require up to the full judgment if the appellee proves by a preponderance that an appellant is dissipating assets outside the ordinary course of business to avoid payment.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-26"},{"t":"Personal sureties must justify","d":"Unless the surety is corporate, sureties must give affidavits of worth totaling double the undertaking. The appellee may except within 10 days after the bond is filed; the appellant then has 10 days, on at least 4 days’ notice, to produce the sureties for examination. If they do not justify in time, the appeal is treated as if no undertaking had been given.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-35"},{"t":"Government appellants","d":"When the state, a county, a municipality, a school district or another listed public body or officer appeals in an official capacity, serving and filing the notice of appeal perfects the appeal and stays the judgment without a bond, though the Supreme Court may require security on motion.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-38"},{"t":"Relief from the Supreme Court","d":"A motion for stay or bond relief may go to the Supreme Court only after showing that asking the circuit court is impracticable or that it denied or failed to give the relief, with its reasons.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-39"}]],["The record and transcript",[{"t":"Order the transcript within 10 days or waive it","d":"Within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must order the transcript in writing on the Supreme Court’s form, serve the order on all parties, and file a copy with the circuit court clerk. Failing to order within the time waives the right to a transcript.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-48, 15-26A-49"},{"t":"Partial transcripts and designations","d":"If ordering less than the whole transcript, the appellant files a statement of issues within the same 10 days. The appellee may designate more within 10 days after service; if the appellant does not order those parts within 10 days, the appellee may order them or move the circuit court within the following 10 days.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-50"},{"t":"Reporter’s 45 days","d":"Make payment arrangements when ordering; if the reporter requires prepayment, pay within 10 days. The transcript is due within 45 days after the reporter receives the order (or the prepayment); extensions come from the Supreme Court clerk.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-51"},{"t":"Clerk’s record","d":"Within 5 days after the notice of appeal is filed, the trial clerk assembles and paginates the record, prepares an alphabetical index, and serves it on counsel and the Supreme Court clerk. Cite the record by these page numbers.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-53, 15-26A-64"},{"t":"No transcript: statement of proceedings or agreed statement","d":"If no report was made or the transcript is unavailable, the appellant may prepare a statement of the proceedings within 15 days after service of the notice of appeal; the appellee has 15 days to object, and the trial court settles it. Alternatively, the parties may sign an agreed statement of the case within 15 days of service of the notice of appeal.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-54, 15-26A-55"}]],["Briefing schedule",[{"t":"45 / 45 / 30 days","d":"The appellant’s brief is due 45 days after service of the notice of appeal if the transcript was obtained before the appeal or none was ordered; otherwise 45 days after service of the transcript or filing of the statement under 15-26A-54 or -55. The appellee’s brief is due 45 days after service of the (last) appellant’s brief, and the reply 30 days after service of the (last) appellee’s brief. In adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals the periods are 25, 25 and 15 days.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-75"},{"t":"One 15-day stipulated extension","d":"The parties may stipulate to one extension of up to 15 days for each initial brief, presented to the Supreme Court clerk before the brief is due. Anything more requires an application to the Chief Justice on notice and good cause.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-76"},{"t":"Missed brief deadlines","d":"If the appellant’s brief is late, the appellee may move to dismiss; a late appellee is not heard at oral argument without permission. The clerk may not accept an untimely brief. Defaults can be excused by stipulation with an affidavit of reasonable excuse, or by the court on application (opposition due in 7 days), only if no dismissal order has been entered.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-77, 15-26A-78, 15-26A-80"},{"t":"Reinstatement after dismissal","d":"A petition to reinstate a dismissed appeal may be filed within 20 days after entry of the dismissal order; answers are due within 10 days after service.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-91"}]],["Brief contents, length and format",[{"t":"Required sections","d":"Table of contents; table of authorities; jurisdictional statement showing the judgment date and form and the notice-of-appeal date; statement of issues, each followed by how the trial court ruled and no more than 4 most relevant cases; statement of the case and facts with record cites; argument; short conclusion; and an appendix with the judgment or order, findings and conclusions, any summary-judgment fact statements, and relevant pleadings, instructions and transcript excerpts (with cover sheets; no summaries).","c":"SDCL 15-26A-60"},{"t":"Length: 32 pages or 10,000 words","d":"In proportional type (serif, 12-point or larger, including footnotes), principal briefs are limited to 32 pages, replies and amicus briefs to 16, and supplemental briefs to 5. A brief may exceed the page limit if it stays within the greater of 10,000 words or 50,000 characters (half that for reply and amicus briefs), with a certificate of compliance stating the count. Monospaced briefs: 40 / 20 / 10 pages. Ask leave to exceed limits at least 10 days before the brief is due.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-66"},{"t":"Format details","d":"Double-spaced, left margin 1½ inches and others at least 1 inch, not right-justified, pages numbered at bottom center; boldface only for captions, section names and argument headings. The cover must give the trial judge’s name and the date the notice of appeal was filed. The clerk may refuse a nonconforming brief, and the party then has 10 days to file a compliant one.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-66(b)(1), 15-26A-69, 15-26A-70"},{"t":"Public-domain citations","d":"Cite South Dakota Supreme Court opinions released on or after January 1, 1996 by year, “S.D.,” and sequential number, with pinpoints to the clerk-assigned paragraph numbers, adding the N.W.2d cite when available.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-69.1"},{"t":"Amicus and supplemental briefs","d":"Amicus briefs need the Court’s request or leave on motion and are due with the brief of the supported party. Supplemental briefs on late authorities, restricted to the new matter, may be filed until the case is called for hearing. Length: 15-26A-73 says 10 pages, but 15-26A-66 allows 10 pages monospaced and only 5 pages proportional, so use the lower figure.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-73, 15-26A-74"}]],["Motions, argument and decision",[{"t":"Motions","d":"Serve motions on all parties and file with proof of service; responses are due within 10 days after service.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-87.2"},{"t":"Appellate attorney fees","d":"Where fees are allowable, a motion with a verified, itemized statement of services must be served and filed before the case is submitted on the merits.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-87.3"},{"t":"Oral argument","d":"Argument is not guaranteed; the Court may decide on the briefs. When argued: appellant 20 minutes, appellee 20, rebuttal 10, and no more than two lawyers per side.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-82, 15-26A-83, 15-26A-85"},{"t":"Summary affirmance or reversal","d":"After briefing, the Court may summarily affirm or reverse by order or memorandum opinion on listed grounds. These dispositions may not be cited as authority except for law of the case, res judicata, collateral estoppel and similar uses.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-87.1"},{"t":"Rehearing: 20 days","d":"A petition for rehearing may be served and filed within 20 days after the formal opinion or summary-disposition order is filed; answers within 10 days after service. State the points overlooked or misapprehended, with record and authority references. Rehearing is the last step in state court; there is no further state review.","c":"SDCL 15-30-4, 15-26A-87.1"},{"t":"Costs and delay damages","d":"The prevailing party recovers listed costs; brief-reproduction and transcript costs only if proof is filed before the decision. On affirmance the Court may add damages for delay of up to 10% of the judgment. Objections to the clerk’s taxation are due within 10 days after mailing of the notice; costs are payable 20 days after notice if no rehearing petition is filed.","c":"SDCL 15-30-6, 15-30-8.1, 15-30-9"}]],["E-filing in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"Odyssey filing is mandatory for lawyers","d":"Attorneys must e-file all documents with the Supreme Court, including petitions, notices of review, motions, briefs and appendices, through Odyssey unless the Court allows otherwise in advance. Registration is consent to electronic service, and filed documents must be served through Odyssey.","c":"SDCL 15-26C-1(1)-(2), 15-26C-4"},{"t":"Formats and the paper original","d":"Documents go in as PDF. A brief is one document, filed in PDF and an approved word-processing format, with the appendix in the same PDF behind a table of contents; electronically filed transcripts are not repeated in the appendix except short excerpts. Lawyers must still submit an original paper brief to the clerk.","c":"SDCL 15-26C-2, 15-26C-3(3), 15-26A-79"},{"t":"11:59 p.m. Central and clerk review","d":"A compliant document submitted by 11:59 p.m. Central time is filed that day. The clerk then accepts or rejects it by Odyssey e-mail; rejection grounds include wrong court, fees not paid or waived, incomplete documents, and rule noncompliance.","c":"SDCL 15-26C-3(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Notice of appeal goes in through the circuit court","d":"The notice of appeal is filed in the circuit court where the judgment was entered (through the circuit-court Odyssey system), and the trial clerk transmits it to the Supreme Court.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-4(4)-(5), 16-21A-2"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (with docketing statement, proof of service, fee) in the circuit court","d":"30 days from judgment or order signed, attested, filed, and written notice of entry given to the adverse party.\nCannot be extended (15-26A-92). Restarts after a timely 15-6-50(b) or 15-6-59 motion is decided and notice of entry given, or after the court’s time to rule expires.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-6, 15-26A-4(4)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (judgment or order signed, attested, filed, and written notice of entry given to the adverse party)","note":"Cannot be extended (15-26A-92). Restarts after a timely 15-6-50(b) or 15-6-59 motion is decided and notice of entry given, or after the court’s time to rule expires."}},{"t":"Renewed JMOL or new-trial motion","d":"10 days from notice of entry of judgment.\nNew-trial motion is deemed denied if not decided (or time extended by order) within 20 days of service and filing.","c":"SDCL 15-6-50(b), 15-6-59(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (notice of entry of judgment)","note":"New-trial motion is deemed denied if not decided (or time extended by order) within 20 days of service and filing."}},{"t":"Cost bond ($500) or consent/indigency affidavit","d":"30 days from same trigger as the notice of appeal.\nFiled with the circuit court clerk within the appeal time.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-23, 15-26A-24","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (same trigger as the notice of appeal)","note":"Filed with the circuit court clerk within the appeal time."}},{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal an intermediate order","d":"10 days from notice of entry of the order.\nFiled with the Supreme Court clerk; 10-page limit. Responses due 7 days after service (15-26A-16).","c":"SDCL 15-26A-13","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (notice of entry of the order)","note":"Filed with the Supreme Court clerk; 10-page limit. Responses due 7 days after service (15-26A-16)."}},{"t":"Notice of review (cross-appeal)","d":"20 days from service of the notice of appeal.\nFiled with the Supreme Court clerk with docketing statement section B and proof of service.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-22","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of appeal)","note":"Filed with the Supreme Court clerk with docketing statement section B and proof of service."}},{"t":"Order transcript","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nLate ordering waives the transcript.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-48, 15-26A-49","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Late ordering waives the transcript."}},{"t":"Statement of proceedings or agreed statement","d":"15 days from service of the notice of appeal.\nOnly when no transcript is available or the parties agree.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-54, 15-26A-55","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of appeal)","note":"Only when no transcript is available or the parties agree."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"45 days from service of the notice of appeal, or service of the transcript if ordered but not received before appeal.\n25 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals. One stipulated 15-day extension (15-26A-76).","c":"SDCL 15-26A-75(1)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of appeal, or service of the transcript if ordered but not received before appeal)","note":"25 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals. One stipulated 15-day extension (15-26A-76)."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"45 days from service of the (last) appellant’s brief.\n25 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-75(2)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (service of the)","note":"25 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"30 days from service of the (last) appellee’s brief.\n15 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals; confined to new matter.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-75(3)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the)","note":"15 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals; confined to new matter."}},{"t":"Request to exceed brief length","d":"10 days from before the brief’s due date.\nCounted backward from the filing date.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-66(c)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (before the brief’s due date)","note":"Counted backward from the filing date."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"10 days from service of the motion.\nUnless the court sets another time.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-87.2","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Unless the court sets another time."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"20 days from filing of the opinion or summary-disposition order.\nAnswer due 10 days after service.","c":"SDCL 15-30-4","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the opinion or summary-disposition order)","note":"Answer due 10 days after service."}},{"t":"Petition to reinstate a dismissed appeal","d":"20 days from entry of the dismissal order.\nAnswer due 10 days after service.","c":"SDCL 15-26A-91","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the dismissal order)","note":"Answer due 10 days after service."}},{"t":"Objections to taxation of appellate costs","d":"10 days from mailing of the clerk’s notice of taxation.\nReplies within 5 days after service of objections.","c":"SDCL 15-30-9","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (mailing of the clerk’s notice of taxation)","note":"Replies within 5 days after service of objections."}},{"t":"Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion","d":"30 days from entry of the order.\nRuns from entry, not notice of entry. Chapter 15-40 enacted by SL 2026, ch 91.","c":"SDCL 15-40-11","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Runs from entry, not notice of entry. Chapter 15-40 enacted by SL 2026, ch 91."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal (with docketing statement, proof of service, fee) in the circuit court",30,"Judgment or order signed, attested, filed, and written notice of entry given to the adverse party","SDCL 15-26A-6, 15-26A-4(4)","Cannot be extended (15-26A-92). Restarts after a timely 15-6-50(b) or 15-6-59 motion is decided and notice of entry given, or after the court’s time to rule expires."],["Renewed JMOL or new-trial motion",10,"Notice of entry of judgment","SDCL 15-6-50(b), 15-6-59(b)","New-trial motion is deemed denied if not decided (or time extended by order) within 20 days of service and filing."],["Cost bond ($500) or consent/indigency affidavit",30,"Same trigger as the notice of appeal","SDCL 15-26A-23, 15-26A-24","Filed with the circuit court clerk within the appeal time."],["Petition for permission to appeal an intermediate order",10,"Notice of entry of the order","SDCL 15-26A-13","Filed with the Supreme Court clerk; 10-page limit. Responses due 7 days after service (15-26A-16)."],["Notice of review (cross-appeal)",20,"Service of the notice of appeal","SDCL 15-26A-22","Filed with the Supreme Court clerk with docketing statement section B and proof of service."],["Order transcript",10,"Filing of the notice of appeal","SDCL 15-26A-48, 15-26A-49","Late ordering waives the transcript."],["Statement of proceedings or agreed statement",15,"Service of the notice of appeal","SDCL 15-26A-54, 15-26A-55","Only when no transcript is available or the parties agree."],["Appellant’s brief",45,"Service of the notice of appeal, or service of the transcript if ordered but not received before appeal","SDCL 15-26A-75(1)","25 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals. One stipulated 15-day extension (15-26A-76)."],["Appellee’s brief",45,"Service of the (last) appellant’s brief","SDCL 15-26A-75(2)","25 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals."],["Reply brief",30,"Service of the (last) appellee’s brief","SDCL 15-26A-75(3)","15 days in adoption and abuse-and-neglect appeals; confined to new matter."],["Request to exceed brief length",10,"Before the brief’s due date","SDCL 15-26A-66(c)","Counted backward from the filing date."],["Response to a motion",10,"Service of the motion","SDCL 15-26A-87.2","Unless the court sets another time."],["Petition for rehearing",20,"Filing of the opinion or summary-disposition order","SDCL 15-30-4","Answer due 10 days after service."],["Petition to reinstate a dismissed appeal",20,"Entry of the dismissal order","SDCL 15-26A-91","Answer due 10 days after service."],["Objections to taxation of appellate costs",10,"Mailing of the clerk’s notice of taxation","SDCL 15-30-9","Replies within 5 days after service of objections."],["Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion",30,"Entry of the order","SDCL 15-40-11","Runs from entry, not notice of entry. Chapter 15-40 enacted by SL 2026, ch 91."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://ujs.sd.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"South Dakota Codified Laws ch. 15-6","rulesSrc":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"15-6-1","t":"Scope of chapter","s":"Chapter 15-6 governs procedure in the circuit courts in all civil suits, subject to the exceptions in § 15-6-81, and is to be construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action”; the distinctions between actions at law and suits in equity, and their old forms, are abolished.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"A civil action is commenced as provided in SDCL 15-2-30 and 15-2-31, not by filing a complaint as under FRCP 3. Under 15-2-30 the action is commenced as to each defendant when the summons is served on that defendant (or on a codefendant united in interest); under 15-2-31, delivering the summons to the sheriff of the proper county with intent that it be served counts as an attempted commencement only if service or first publication follows within 60 days. This controls statutes of limitations.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-4","t":"Summons and service of process","s":"The summons must require the defendant to answer and serve the answer within 30 days after service (excluding the day of service), and the complaint need not be served with it; if it is not, a defendant who appears and demands the complaint in writing within 30 days must be served with it within 20 days, and then has 30 days to answer. Service may be made by a sheriff, constable, or any non-party who is an elector of any state; personal service rules cover business entities (officer, director, registered agent, or person in charge of an in-state office), counties, municipalities, townships, school districts, minors (parent or custodian, and the minor personally if over 14), incompetent persons, the state (designated officer plus the attorney general), state officers (certified mail to the attorney general with an admission of service), and foreign defendants (Hague Convention or other listed means); substitute service at the dwelling with a resident over 14 counts as personal service if the defendant cannot conveniently be found. Proof is by sheriff’s certificate, affidavit, written admission, or publication affidavits; process may be amended; and service by mail with the § 15-6-4(j) notice-and-admission form is allowed, but a defendant who does not return the admission within 20 days of mailing without good cause must pay the costs of personal service (unlike FRCP 4(d), mailing is completed by the signed admission, not a waiver).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Later pleadings, written motions, briefs, notices, demands, offers of judgment, and similar papers must be served on every party (parties in default need not be served, except with new or added claims, which must be served like a summons). Service on a represented party goes to the attorney; documents filed or served through the Odyssey e-filing system are presumed served on all attorneys of record; other papers may be served on attorneys through Odyssey, by email (designated address, or the State Bar directory address), first-class mail (complete on mailing), fax (only by agreement or prior fax service), or office delivery; unrepresented parties are served by delivery, dwelling service on a resident over 14, first-class mail, or, if no address is known, leaving it with the clerk. Originals must be filed “forthwith” after service, and a notice of entry of judgment or notice of filing of an order not filed within 10 days after service is deemed served on its filing date; filing must be through Odyssey unless exempted (self-represented parties may file on paper); depositions, interrogatories, document requests, admissions requests, and responses are not filed except as exhibits or trial designations (designated depositions are filed in full at designation). A civil case filing statement with identifying information on all parties must be filed when commencing an action, appearing, or filing a first responsive pleading, and whenever a party is added; on written request, discovery requests under §§ 15-6-31, -33, -34, and -36 must also be served by email or on a storage device, or the court orders reproduction costs. Subdivisions (f) and (j) are repealed.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, legal holiday (§ 1-5-1), or (for filing) a day the clerk’s office is inaccessible; for periods under 11 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded (the pre-2009 federal method, not FRCP 6’s count-every-day rule). Fax, email, and Odyssey service must be completed by 11:59 p.m. Central on a non-holiday weekday or is deemed made the next such weekday; 3 days are added for mail service only, not for fax or email. Written motions and notices of hearing must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, opposing affidavits or briefs at least 5 days before, and reply papers at least 2 days before, unless the court orders otherwise; the court may enlarge time before expiry or, on motion after expiry, for excusable neglect, but not for §§ 15-6-50(b), 15-6-59(b) and (d), and 15-6-60(b). Subdivision (c) is superseded.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"The pleadings are a complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party complaint, and third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and set out the relief sought; exhibits to motions are subject to Rule 11 and the protective provisions of § 15-6-26(c). Demurrers, pleas, and exceptions are abolished.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment, and relief may be demanded in the alternative. Answers must admit or deny (lack of knowledge operates as a denial) and must plead listed affirmative defenses, including contributory negligence, assumption of risk, statute of limitations, statute of frauds, release, and res judicata; averments other than the amount of damages are admitted if not denied. Pleadings must be simple and direct, may be alternative or inconsistent, and are construed to do substantial justice.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity need not be pleaded, but a challenge to capacity or legal existence requires a specific negative averment; fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while intent and other states of mind may be pleaded generally. Conditions precedent may be pleaded generally but denied specifically; official documents, judgments, statutes (by number), and ordinances may be pleaded in short form; time and place are material; and special damages must be specifically stated. Unknown opposing parties may be named by any name and later substituted, and a libel or slander complaint need only allege generally that the statement was published or spoken about the plaintiff.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Each pleading needs a caption with the court, county, title, and § 15-6-7(a) designation (all parties in the complaint), with averments in numbered paragraphs and separate counts where that aids clarity; statements may be adopted by reference, and exhibits are part of the pleading. A South Dakota addition: Roman numerals may not be used to number paragraphs, and all filed papers (except exhibits) must be typed or hand printed in at least 12-point type on one side of 8½ x 11 inch white paper, with pages numbered at the bottom center.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-11","t":"Signing of pleadings; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by an attorney of record (or an unrepresented party) with address and phone number; pleadings need not be verified unless a rule or statute says so, and unsigned papers are stricken unless promptly corrected. Signing certifies proper purpose, legal warrant, and evidentiary support as in FRCP 11(b); a sanctions motion must be separate and may not be filed until 21 days after service if the challenged paper is not withdrawn or corrected (safe harbor); sanctions are limited to deterrence, with no monetary sanctions against a represented party for legal contentions or on the court’s own initiative after voluntary dismissal or settlement, and the rule does not apply to discovery. South Dakota addition: the Supreme Court reviews Rule 11 appeals without any presumption that the trial court’s findings and conclusions are correct, and the successful party on appeal is awarded reasonable attorney fees and costs.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-12","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"The answer is due 30 days after service of the complaint (not 21 as under FRCP 12); an answer to a cross-claim and a reply to a counterclaim are due in 20 days; after denial or postponement of a Rule 12 motion, the responsive pleading is due in 10 days after notice, in 10 days after service of a more definite statement, and in 20 days after a reversal of a dismissal order is filed in the trial court. The defenses of lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, insufficient process or service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a § 15-6-19 party may be raised by pre-answer motion (there is no improper-venue defense in the list); a failure-to-state-a-claim motion or motion for judgment on the pleadings that relies on matters outside the pleadings is treated as summary judgment. A more definite statement ordered by the court must be provided within 10 days of notice; a motion to strike is due before responding or within 20 days if no response is permitted; omitted personal-jurisdiction and service defenses are waived; failure to state a claim and failure to join an indispensable party may be raised through trial; and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction requires dismissal at any time.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"Counterclaims arising from the same transaction or occurrence are compulsory, unless already pending in another action, the opposing claim was by attachment without personal jurisdiction, or the court would lack jurisdiction over the claim as an original action; other counterclaims are permissive and may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. The chapter does not enlarge counterclaims against the State of South Dakota; later-matured claims may be added by supplemental pleading with leave; omitted counterclaims may be added by amendment with leave for oversight, inadvertence, excusable neglect, or when justice requires; and cross-claims against co-parties must arise from the same transaction or occurrence or concern the same property. Additional parties may be joined under §§ 15-6-19 and 15-6-20, and separate judgments may enter under § 15-6-54(b).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may serve a third-party summons and complaint on a non-party who is or may be liable to it, without leave if served within 10 days after serving its original answer (FRCP 14 now allows 14 days), and otherwise only with leave on motion. The third-party defendant may raise the third-party plaintiff’s defenses against the plaintiff and assert related claims against the plaintiff, and vice versa; any party may move to strike, sever, or separately try the third-party claim. South Dakota addition: among joint tortfeasors with a judgment in one action, the contribution rule of § 15-8-15 applies only if proportionate fault was litigated between them by cross-claim, counterclaim, or third-party claim in that action; a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may also implead.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as a matter of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after serving a pleading that needs no response if the case is not on the trial calendar or set for trial; otherwise amendment requires leave (freely given) or written consent. The response to an amended pleading is due within the remaining original response time or 10 days after service, whichever is longer. Issues tried by consent are treated as pleaded and amendments to conform may come even after judgment; amendments relate back when they arise from the same conduct, transaction, or occurrence, and a change of party relates back only if, within the limitations period, the new party had notice and knew or should have known it would have been sued but for a mistake (no separate FRCP 4(m) service window); supplemental pleadings are allowed on motion.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-16","t":"Pretrial procedure: scheduling order","s":"Before trial the court, on its own or a party’s motion and after consulting counsel and unrepresented parties, must enter a scheduling order limiting time to join parties and amend, file and hear motions, and complete discovery, and setting conference, final pretrial conference, and trial dates. The schedule may be modified only by leave of the judge for good cause. The South Dakota rule is a short version: it does not contain FRCP 16’s detailed provisions on pretrial conferences, pretrial orders, or sanctions.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-17","t":"Parties; capacity","s":"Actions must be brought by the real party in interest, though representatives such as personal representatives, guardians, conservators, bailees, and trustees may sue in their own names, and dismissal for that defect must await a reasonable time for ratification, joinder, or substitution. South Dakota capacity rule: persons doing business under a common name may sue or be sued by that name with service on one or more associates, and the judgment binds the associates’ joint property and the individual property of those served. Minors and incompetent persons sue or defend through a guardian or conservator or a court-appointed guardian ad litem, who may not receive the ward’s money without approved security and may settle with court approval.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party. Claims formerly allowed only after another claim concluded may be joined, so a creditor may sue for money and to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first obtaining a money judgment.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"A person subject to service must be joined if complete relief cannot be given without the person, or if the person’s absence would impair the person’s interest or expose parties to inconsistent obligations; a joined party whose joinder makes venue improper and who objects must be dismissed. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed or dismiss, using four listed factors, and a claimant must plead the names of required persons not joined and the reasons. The rule is subject to the class-action rule, § 15-6-23.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, when claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact; judgment may be given for or against any of them according to their rights and liabilities. The court may order separate trials or other orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, expense, or prejudice.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal. Parties may be dropped or added by court order at any stage on just terms, and any claim against a party may be severed.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Claimants against a plaintiff who is exposed to double or multiple liability may be joined as defendants and required to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability; a defendant facing similar exposure may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim. The rule supplements § 15-6-20; the text contains no counterpart to FRCP 22(b)’s reference to statutory interpleader.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-23","t":"Class actions","s":"A class action requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy, plus one of the three FRCP 23(b) types; South Dakota adds a fifth prerequisite barring class suits against the state to recover taxes imposed by chapters 10-39, 10-39A, 10-43, 10-44, 10-45, 10-46, 10-46A, 10-46B, or 10-52. The court decides certification as soon as practicable, may make it conditional, must give (b)(3) members the best practicable notice with the right to opt out, and may certify issue classes and subclasses. Dismissal or compromise requires court approval and notice to the class; the text lacks the later federal provisions on settlement standards, objectors, class counsel, fee awards, and interlocutory appeal of certification orders (FRCP 23(e)–(h) as amended).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-23.1","t":"Derivative actions by unincorporated associations","s":"A member’s derivative action to enforce a right of an unincorporated association needs a verified complaint alleging membership at the time of the transaction (or by operation of law), that the suit is not collusive to create jurisdiction, and the demand efforts made (or why none was made). The suit fails if the plaintiff does not fairly represent similarly situated members, and dismissal or compromise needs court approval and notice to members. Unlike FRCP 23.1, the text addresses only unincorporated associations, not corporations.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"An action by or against association members as a class through named representatives may proceed only if the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the association and its members. The court may make orders like those under § 15-6-23(d), and dismissal or compromise follows § 15-6-23(e).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right is allowed on timely application when a state statute confers an unconditional right or when the applicant’s interest may be impaired and is not adequately represented; permissive intervention is allowed for a common question of law or fact, including by government officers or agencies when a party relies on a statute or regulation they administer. The intervenor must serve a motion stating the grounds with a pleading setting out the claim or defense. A party challenging the constitutionality of a South Dakota legislative act in a case where the state is not a party must notify the attorney general in time to allow intervention.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record, or the action is dismissed as to the decedent; the motion is served on parties under § 15-6-5 and on non-parties like a summons. The court may continue the action against an incompetent party’s guardian, conservator, or guardian ad litem, and after a transfer of interest the action continues against the original party unless the court orders substitution or joinder; a successor to a public or corporate officer, personal representative, or trustee is automatically substituted. South Dakota addition: when a party who won a judgment for possession of real property dies, the successor in interest may revive and enforce it on motion within 1 year after the death, or later by supplemental complaint.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-25.1","t":"Dismissal of parties: later pleadings","s":"Once a party is dismissed or dropped from a multiparty action by court order, that party’s name may not appear on later pleadings. This has no FRCP counterpart.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Discovery methods are depositions, interrogatories, production and entry requests, examinations, and requests for admission, with frequency not limited unless the court orders otherwise; the scope remains the older standard of any non-privileged matter “relevant to the subject matter” and “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” not the federal proportionality standard, though the court must limit cumulative, duplicative, or unduly burdensome discovery. Insurance agreements are discoverable; work product needs substantial need and undue hardship; expert discovery proceeds by interrogatories identifying testifying experts, their subject matter, facts, opinions, and grounds, plus their depositions, with drafts and most attorney-expert communications protected and non-testifying experts discoverable only in exceptional circumstances; privilege withholding must be expressly claimed and described. Protective-order motions require a good-faith conferral certification; discovery may proceed in any sequence; and responses must be supplemented when materially incomplete or incorrect, including expert reports, answers, and deposition testimony. South Dakota has no initial-disclosure, expert-report, or discovery-planning-conference requirement in this text (subdivision (f) is superseded).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A person expecting to be a party to an action who cannot yet bring it may file a verified petition in the circuit court of the proper county to perpetuate testimony, serving notice on expected adverse parties at least 20 days before the hearing in the manner of a summons (with publication and appointed counsel for those not served); the court orders depositions if perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice. After judgment, while an appeal is pending or the time to appeal has not expired, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony on motion. The rule does not limit an independent action to perpetuate testimony.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In the United States, depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court-appointed person; abroad, by treaty, letter of request, on notice before an authorized person, or before a commissioned person. South Dakota adds a detailed disqualification rule: no deposition may be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or of counsel, or anyone financially interested, and “employee” includes reporters with contractual relationships to anyone interested in the outcome, such as an insurer or third-party administrator. The officer may not enter reporting agreements that give up control of the original transcript before certification, give special terms or incentives to one side, allow others to set rates, or place the reporter on a preferred-provider list under a multi-case contract; these provisions cannot be waived except by a waiver request in the deposition notice, and a deposition taken in violation is void.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-28.1","t":"Interstate depositions and discovery: scope","s":"Sections 15-6-28.1 to 15-6-28.6 (South Dakota’s version of the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act) govern depositions and discovery in South Dakota for civil suits pending in another state.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-28.2","t":"Interstate discovery: definitions","s":"Defines “foreign jurisdiction” (another state), “foreign subpoena,” “person,” “state” (including D.C., Puerto Rico, the U.S. Virgin Islands, federally recognized Indian tribes, and U.S. territories), and “subpoena” (to testify, produce documents or electronically stored information, or permit inspection of premises).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-28.3","t":"Interstate discovery: issuing the subpoena","s":"A party submits the foreign subpoena to the clerk of court in the county where discovery is sought, and the clerk promptly issues a South Dakota subpoena that conforms to § 15-6-45, advises the recipient of the right to move to quash or modify, and lists all counsel of record and unrepresented parties with contact information. Submitting the request is not an appearance, but it gives South Dakota courts jurisdiction to enforce, quash, or modify the subpoena, issue protective orders, and sanction the requesting attorney for rule violations. An attorney not licensed in South Dakota may litigate enforcement or other disputes over the subpoena without pro hac vice admission.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-28.4","t":"Interstate discovery: service","s":"A subpoena issued by the clerk under §§ 15-6-28.1 to 15-6-28.6 must be served as required by § 15-6-45(c).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-28.5","t":"Interstate discovery: other subpoena provisions","s":"All other provisions of § 15-6-45 (on depositions, production, inspection, witness fees, expenses, place of examination, and required attendance) apply to subpoenas issued under §§ 15-6-28.1 to 15-6-28.6.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-28.6","t":"Interstate discovery: applications to court","s":"Motions for protective orders or to enforce, quash, or modify a clerk-issued interstate subpoena must follow South Dakota law and be filed in the court of the county where the discovery is to be conducted.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-29","t":"Stipulations about depositions","s":"If the parties stipulate in writing, depositions may be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner, and used like other depositions. Unlike FRCP 29, the text does not address stipulations modifying other discovery procedures.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-30","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Any party may depose any person after commencement, but the plaintiff needs leave to notice a deposition within 30 days after service of the summons and complaint on any defendant unless a defendant has begun discovery or a special notice certifies that the witness is about to leave the state or country; deposing a prisoner, or anyone already deposed in the case, requires leave or (for a repeat deposition) consent of the deponent and parties. Notice must be reasonable and written and state the recording method (sound, sound-and-visual, or stenographic, at the noticing party’s cost); organizations named under (b)(6) must designate witnesses; and remote depositions require a written stipulation or court order. Objections must be concise and nonargumentative, instructions not to answer are limited to privilege, court-ordered limits, or a motion to terminate, and a deposition is limited to one day of 7 hours; the witness has 15 days after submission to read and sign, the officer sends the certified electronic original to the attorney who arranged the transcript, and a party who fails to attend its own noticed deposition or to subpoena the witness may be ordered to pay others’ expenses and fees.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-31","t":"Depositions by written questions","s":"Any party may take a deposition on written questions after commencement, with leave needed for prisoners and consent or leave needed for persons already deposed; the notice must identify the deponent and the officer, and organizations may be deposed under § 15-6-30(b)(6). Cross-questions are due within 14 days after the notice and questions are served, redirect questions within 7 days after cross-questions, and recross-questions within 7 days after redirect, subject to court change. The officer takes and certifies the answers, and the filing party must give prompt notice of filing; subdivision (d) is superseded.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-32","t":"Use of depositions; errors and irregularities","s":"Depositions may be used against parties who were present, represented, or had notice: for impeachment or any purpose the rules of evidence allow; an adverse party’s, officer’s, or designee’s deposition for any purpose; and any witness’s deposition if the witness is dead, out of the state (rather than FRCP 32’s more than 100 miles away), unable to attend because of age, illness, infirmity, imprisonment, or occupational commitments (if taken for trial use for that reason), unreachable by subpoena, or in exceptional circumstances. Rule-of-completeness and prior-action provisions apply, objections to admissibility may be made at trial, and taking a deposition does not make the deponent one’s own witness. Notice defects are waived without prompt written objection; officer disqualification without objection before or promptly after discovery; curable errors at the deposition without timely objection; objections to the form of written questions unless served within the time for the next questions and within 5 days after the last questions; and transcription and handling defects without a prompt motion to suppress.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of the summons and complaint; the text sets no numerical limit (FRCP 33 caps them at 25), and § 15-6-26(a) says frequency is not limited unless the court orders otherwise. Answers under oath and objections are due within 30 days after service, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of the summons and complaint; time may be changed by court order or written agreement. Objections must be specific or are waived absent good cause, each interrogatory must be restated before its answer, contention interrogatories are permitted but may be deferred, and a party may answer by specifying business records when the burden is substantially the same.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-34","t":"Production of documents and things; entry on land","s":"A party may request inspection, copying, testing, or sampling of documents and tangible things in another party’s possession, custody, or control, or entry on land, with items described with reasonable particularity and a reasonable time, place, and manner specified. Requests may be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after the summons and complaint, and the written response is due in 30 days (45 days after service of the summons and complaint for a defendant), stating for each item whether inspection will be permitted or the reasons for objection. Documents must be produced as kept in the usual course of business or organized by request category; the text has no FRCP 34 provisions on the form of electronically stored information. The rule does not bar an independent action against a non-party.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-35","t":"Physical and mental examinations and blood tests","s":"When a party’s mental or physical condition, or a party’s blood relationship (consanguinity) with another, is in controversy, the court may order an examination or blood test by a physician on motion for good cause and notice, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. The examined party may request the physician’s detailed written report, after which the requesting side is entitled to like reports of other examinations; a physician who fails to report may be barred from testifying. Requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to all others who examine the same condition; the rule also applies to examinations by agreement.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A party may serve requests to admit facts, the application of law to fact, opinions of fact, or the genuineness of documents, on the plaintiff after commencement and on other parties with or after the summons and complaint. Each matter is admitted unless a written, signed answer or objection is served within 30 days after service (a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint), and the text sets no numerical limit; denials must fairly meet the substance, lack of knowledge requires a stated reasonable inquiry, and a genuine issue for trial is not, by itself, a ground to object. The requesting party may move to test sufficiency, and admitted matters are conclusively established for that action only unless the court permits withdrawal or amendment.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-37","t":"Failure to make discovery; sanctions","s":"A motion to compel goes to the court where the action is pending (or, for a non-party, the circuit where discovery is taken) and must certify a good-faith attempt to confer; evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and the court must award expenses and fees to the winner unless substantially justified, with apportionment on mixed results. Disobeying a discovery order may lead to facts deemed established, preclusion, striking pleadings, stay, dismissal, default, or contempt (except for an examination order), plus mandatory expenses; failing to supplement under § 15-6-26(e) bars use of the undisclosed witness or information unless harmless, and failing to admit something later proved shifts the cost of proof unless an exception applies. A party who fails entirely to attend its own deposition, answer interrogatories, or respond to a production request may be sanctioned without a prior order, and objections are no excuse unless a protective order was sought. The text has no counterpart to FRCP 37(e) on lost electronically stored information or 37(f) on discovery plans.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"The jury right under S.D. Const. art. VI, § 6 or a South Dakota statute is preserved. A party demands a jury by serving a written demand (it may be endorsed on a pleading) no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue; a demand naming only some issues lets any other party demand a jury on other issues within 10 days after service of that demand. Failing to serve a demand waives the jury, and a demand may not be withdrawn without the parties’ consent.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties consent to a bench trial by written stipulation filed or oral stipulation in open court, a party waives by not appearing or by going to trial to the court without objecting, or the court finds no jury right exists. The court may still order a jury on a late motion, may use an advisory jury in nonjury actions, and with both parties’ consent may use a jury whose verdict binds as if the right existed.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial","s":"The judges set the trial calendar by order or rule, giving precedence to cases entitled to it. Subdivision (b) was repealed (SL 1993, ch 386). Once a case is reached on the calendar, a party may proceed in the adverse party’s absence and take a dismissal, verdict, or judgment unless the court for good cause directs otherwise.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by notice before the adverse party serves an answer or a summary-judgment motion (whichever comes first), or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; dismissal is without prejudice except that a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise dismissal at the plaintiff’s request requires a court order, and not over the objection of a defendant whose earlier counterclaim cannot stand independently. A defendant may move to dismiss for failure to prosecute or to comply with the rules or an order, and may move for dismissal after the plaintiff rests in a bench trial (findings required under 15-6-52(a)); unless the order says otherwise, an involuntary dismissal (other than for lack of jurisdiction or failure to join a party under 15-6-19) is on the merits. The rule applies to counterclaims, cross-claims, and third-party claims, and a refiling plaintiff can be ordered to pay costs of the earlier dismissed action, with a stay until paid.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"The court may order joint hearings or trials, or consolidate actions that share a common question of law or fact, to avoid unnecessary cost or delay. It may order separate trials of any claim or issue for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for expedition and economy, preserving the right to a jury under the state or federal constitution or a statute.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-43","t":"Evidence","s":"Trial testimony is taken orally in open court unless this chapter or the South Dakota Rules of Evidence provide otherwise; subdivisions (b) and (c) are superseded. Oaths may follow §§ 19-19-603.1 and 19-19-603.2, motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, oral testimony, or depositions, and on a party’s motion a hearing under § 21-1-4.1 must be decided before trial. Interpreters are appointed and paid under § 19-3-7.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"A domestic official record may be proved by an official publication or a copy certified by its legal custodian or deputy; a foreign record by official publication or an attested copy with a final certification by a U.S. or foreign diplomatic or consular official, which the court may excuse for good cause when parties had a reasonable chance to investigate. A written statement of diligent search, authenticated the same way, proves the absence of a record, and other lawful methods of proof remain available.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"A party raising an issue of a foreign country’s law must give notice in the pleadings or other reasonable written notice. The court may consider any relevant source, admissible or not, and its ruling is treated as a ruling on a question of law.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Subpoenas may be issued by clerks, judges, magistrates, notaries, referees, and authorized agencies, and by any active, in-good-standing South Dakota attorney of record, who must contemporaneously send a copy to the clerk (or tribunal’s filing officer) for filing; every subpoena must carry a required bold, capitalized warning above the signature. Before a document subpoena is served, notice and a copy must be served on every party; the court may quash or modify an unreasonable and oppressive subpoena on a prompt motion made by the compliance time, or condition denial on advance payment of production costs. Service is made like a summons (no publication), far enough ahead to allow travel, with one day’s attendance fee and mileage tendered (not required for the state or its subdivisions), and the witness may demand each later day’s fee; a deposition subpoena recipient may serve written objections to inspection within 10 days after service (or by the compliance time if sooner), after which a court order is needed. A resident may be required to attend a deposition only in the county of residence, employment, or business, and a nonresident served in South Dakota only in the county of service (unless the court fixes another place); disobedience may be contempt, and subdivision (e) is superseded.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are not needed; it is enough to make known the desired action or objection and the grounds when the ruling is made or sought. A party with no opportunity to object is not prejudiced by the lack of an objection.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-47","t":"Jurors","s":"The court must permit the parties or their attorneys to conduct voir dire. The court may seat up to 6 alternates, who replace jurors in order before deliberations and are discharged when the jury retires; each side gets 1 extra peremptory challenge for 1–2 alternates, 2 for 3–4, and 3 for 5–6, usable only against alternates. The court may excuse a juror for good cause during trial or deliberation.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-48","t":"Juries of fewer than twelve; majority verdict","s":"The parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than 12 or to accept a verdict or finding of a stated majority of jurors.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"The court may require a special verdict with written findings on each fact issue; an omitted fact issue is waived for jury trial unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires, and the court may find it or is deemed to have found it consistent with the judgment. With a general verdict plus interrogatories, judgment enters under 15-6-58 when they agree; if answers are consistent with each other but conflict with the verdict, the court may enter judgment on the answers, return the jury, or order a new trial, and if the answers conflict with each other and the verdict, judgment may not enter.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; renewed motion","s":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law (still titled ‘directed verdict and judgment notwithstanding the verdict’) may be made any time before submission and must state the judgment sought and the supporting law and facts. A renewed motion must be filed no later than 10 days after notice of entry of judgment, and may join or alternatively request a new trial under 15-6-59; the court may let the judgment stand, order a new trial, or direct judgment. If the renewed motion is granted the court must conditionally rule on any new-trial motion, a party against whom judgment as a matter of law is rendered has 10 days after entry of the judgment to move for a new trial, and a party who defeated the motion may assert new-trial grounds as respondent on appeal.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-51","t":"Jury instructions; objections","s":"Parties file written instruction requests at the close of evidence or an earlier time set by the court, with later requests allowed for unanticipated issues or by permission. The court must tell the parties its proposed instructions and rulings on requests before instructing and before final arguments, and give an on-the-record chance to object outside the jury’s hearing. Objections must distinctly state the matter and grounds; error may be assigned only on a proper objection (or a proper request plus objection, unless the court definitively rejected the request on the record), though the court may consider plain error affecting substantial rights.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In bench and advisory-jury trials the court must find facts specially and state conclusions of law separately (also when granting or refusing a TRO or preliminary injunction, except in chapters 25-3 and 25-4 actions); findings are set aside only if clearly erroneous. Findings may be oral on the record or in a memorandum, or the court may direct prevailing counsel to serve proposed findings, conclusions, and judgment within 10 days after the decision is announced; the court may not sign them until 5 days after service, during which parties may serve objections or their own proposals, and a failure to find on a material issue is not preserved unless the finding was proposed or requested. Findings are unnecessary on 15-6-12 or 15-6-56 motions or other motions (except 15-6-41(b)), and are waived by failing to appear, written or oral consent, or a stipulation of facts.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-53","t":"Referees","s":"A court may appoint a referee who is not disqualified as a judge would be (unless parties consent after disclosure); the court fixes and allocates pay, and an unpaid referee may get a writ of execution; in chapter 25-7A support cases the referee may recommend fees and costs and is paid by the court. References are the exception: in jury cases only for complicated issues, and in nonjury cases only for accounts, difficult damages computations, or an exceptional condition. The referee sets a first meeting within 20 days after the order of reference, may subpoena witnesses and rule on evidence, and files a report; in nonjury actions the court accepts findings unless clearly erroneous and any party may serve written objections within 10 days after notice of the filing, while in jury actions findings may be read to the jury, and stipulated-final findings leave only questions of law.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-54","t":"Judgments; costs; attorney fees","s":"A judgment is the final determination of the parties’ rights (other written directions are orders), and in multi-claim or multi-party cases a partial final judgment requires an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. Default judgments are limited in kind and amount to the demand; other judgments grant the relief earned. Costs (not fees) go to the prevailing party on a verified, itemized application for taxation filed with the clerk and served; objections with a notice of hearing are due within 10 days of service or the costs are deemed agreed, and costs are waived unless applied for within 30 days of entry of judgment (extendable for good cause). Attorney fees are sought by motion filed no later than 14 days after entry of judgment unless a statute or order says otherwise, stating the grounds and amount; this procedure does not apply to sanctions.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-55","t":"Default","s":"A party’s failure to plead or otherwise defend must be shown by affidavit, and every default judgment is entered by application to the court (there is no clerk’s default judgment); no default may enter against a minor or incompetent person without an appeared guardian, conservator, or guardian ad litem. A party who has appeared must be served with written notice of the application at least 3 days before the hearing, and no default may be rendered until a complaint has been on file at least 20 days unless it was served with the summons; after publication service the court may require security for restitution. The court may set aside a default judgment for good cause under 15-6-60(b), and the rule applies to all claimants subject to 15-6-54(c).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move any time after 30 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves), and a defending party may move at any time. Unless the court orders otherwise, the motion, brief, statement of undisputed material facts, and affidavits must be served at least 28 calendar days before the hearing, responses (including a numbered response to each fact with record cites) at least 14 calendar days before, and any reply at least 7 calendar days before, with no weekend/holiday exclusion for the 7-day period; facts not controverted in the opposing statement are admitted. Judgment issues if there is no genuine issue of material fact, may be rendered on liability alone, and when not fully granted the court should specify facts established for trial; affidavits must be on personal knowledge, the opponent must set out specific facts, the court may continue the motion for discovery, and bad-faith affidavits draw expenses, fees, and possible contempt.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory judgments under chapter 21-24 follow this chapter, with a jury available under 15-6-38 and 15-6-39. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may order a speedy hearing and advance the case.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-58","t":"Entry of judgment and orders","s":"Judgment is promptly rendered, every judgment must be on a separate document, and a judgment or order becomes effective only when written, signed, attested by the clerk, and filed; the clerk dockets it at once (divorce and foreclosure judgments are docketed ‘see file’), and entry is not delayed for costs. Under subdivision (b) (added by Supreme Court Rule 25-07), a party directed to prepare an order or judgment without findings must send a proposed version to all parties within 5 days; parties then have 5 days to confer, an agreed or unopposed draft is marked ‘NO OBJECTION AS TO FORM BY COUNSEL,’ and if there is no agreement each party submits its own proposal within 2 days after the confer period. Failing to submit a timely proposal waives form objections unless excused for good cause, and the court may modify the procedure. The collected text of this section contains no notice-of-entry provision, though 15-6-50 and 15-6-59 run from notice of entry.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-59","t":"New trials; amendment of judgments","s":"Grounds are irregularity or abuse of discretion preventing a fair trial, jury misconduct (including chance verdicts, provable by a juror’s affidavit), accident or surprise, newly discovered evidence, damages from passion or prejudice, insufficient evidence or a decision against law, and preserved errors of law; grounds (1)–(4) require affidavits unless (1) appears of record, and insufficiency claims must state particulars. The motion must be served and filed no later than 10 days after notice of entry of judgment; the court must rule within 20 days after service and filing or extend that time by order within the 20 days, or the motion is deemed denied. The court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days after entry of judgment, must specify every ground when granting (unspecified grounds are deemed overruled), and in bench cases may instead amend findings and judgment. A new-trial motion is not required to appeal on grounds (6) and (7) if the matter was presented to the trial court as provided in § 15-26A-8.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time; during an appeal, before the settled record goes to the Supreme Court clerk, and afterward only with the Supreme Court’s leave. Relief from a final judgment is available for mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud or misconduct, a void judgment, satisfaction or changed circumstances, or any other justifying reason; the motion must be made within a reasonable time and, for reasons (1)–(3), not more than 1 year after entry. The motion does not affect finality or suspend the judgment, and independent actions and relief for fraud on the court remain available.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-61","t":"Harmless error","s":"No error in evidence rulings or other acts justifies a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusing relief would be inconsistent with substantial justice. Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise for good cause, or on a default judgment, no execution or enforcement may proceed until 30 days after entry; injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed unless the court orders. The court may stay enforcement pending motions under 15-6-50, 15-6-59, or 15-6-60 on security conditions, and may stay a 15-6-54(b) partial judgment until later judgments. Injunctions pending appeal, stays on appeal, and stays for the state are governed by chapter 15-26A, and the Supreme Court’s power to stay or preserve the status quo is not limited.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-63","t":"Disability of a judge","s":"If the trial judge dies, is sick or disabled, or leaves office after a verdict or after findings and conclusions are filed, another judge sitting in or assigned to the court may finish the post-trial duties. A successor who cannot perform them, because of not presiding at trial or otherwise, may grant a new trial.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"All state-law remedies for seizing a person or property to secure a future judgment are available at the start of and during an action, under the circumstances and in the manner state law provides.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-65","t":"Injunctions and restraining orders","s":"No preliminary injunction issues without notice, and the court may consolidate the merits trial with the hearing (preserving jury rights). A TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable harm and the attorney’s written certification of notice efforts; it must expire within the time the court sets, not over 10 days, unless extended for good cause for a like period or by consent (chapters 25-3 and 25-4 actions excepted), the adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice, and the text states that TROs ‘may not be appealed.’ Security is required as the court sets (not from the state), and every order must state reasons, describe the restrained acts in reasonable detail without reference to other documents, bind only parties and those in active concert with actual notice, and for preliminary injunctions include findings and conclusions.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-66","t":"Receivers","s":"An action in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order. Estate administration by receivers follows statute and prior state practice; otherwise this chapter governs.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"By leave of court and on notice, a party may deposit money or a deliverable thing in dispute with the court. A defendant sued on a contract or for specific property may, before answering and on affidavit of an adverse nonparty claim, seek substitution of the claimant and discharge by depositing the debt or property; a bailee facing adverse claims with no action pending may apply to the circuit judge to designate a depository and is discharged after notifying claimants personally or by registered or certified mail. The court may order a party who admits holding money or property for another to deposit or deliver it, enforceable by contempt and the sheriff, and voluntary partial payments after judgment are credited against it.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"Any party (not only a defending party) may serve an offer of judgment more than 10 days before trial; the offeree has 10 days after service to accept in writing, after which either party files the offer and acceptance and the clerk enters judgment. An unaccepted offer is deemed withdrawn and is inadmissible except on costs; if the offeree’s final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. After liability is determined, the party held liable may make an offer served a reasonable time but not less than 10 days before the damages hearing.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-69","t":"Execution","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, under the applicable statutes and rules. The judgment creditor (or a successor of record) may take discovery from any person, including the debtor, in aid of the judgment.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to convey land, deliver documents, or perform a specific act within the time set, the court may appoint someone to do it at the party’s cost, the clerk issues a writ of attachment or sequestration on application, and contempt is available. For property in the state the court may simply divest and vest title by judgment, and a party entitled to possession gets a writ of execution or assistance from the clerk.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-71","t":"Process for and against nonparties","s":"A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it as if a party, and a nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same process as a party.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-72","t":"Expedited civil actions: general provisions","s":"Article IX expedited civil actions are money-only cases in which all claims by or against any one party total $75,000 or less, including damages, penalties, and attorney fees but excluding prejudgment interest, post-judgment interest, and costs; small claims and domestic relations cases are excluded. A plaintiff elects the track by a Supreme Court form certification (Form 27) signed by all plaintiffs and their attorneys, which is inadmissible to prove damages, and judgments are capped at $75,000 (the jury is not told of the cap); parties in other cases may jointly move into the track (Form 28) and then are not bound by the cap unless they agree. On timely application the court may terminate the track for substantially changed circumstances or good cause, or for a good-faith compulsory counterclaim beyond these limits; permissive counterclaims are capped unless severed, and a ‘side’ means all litigants with generally common interests. The regular rules otherwise apply.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-72.1","t":"Local intergovernmental disputes","s":"When two or more local government bodies are in a legal dispute, the court on motion or on its own may order them into the expedited civil action process of 15-6-73 to 15-6-76.1 regardless of amount, or into alternative dispute resolution other than binding arbitration.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-72.2","t":"Local government body defined","s":"For 15-6-72.1, local government bodies include counties, municipalities, townships, and unorganized territory; school districts; public-service organizations they organize or authorize (law enforcement, fire, emergency medical); and listed special districts (for example ambulance, conservation, consumer power, irrigation, sanitary, water, and watershed districts).","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-73","t":"Discovery in expedited civil actions","s":"Unless the parties agree or the court grants leave for good cause on a written motion stating the proposed discovery, all discovery must be finished no later than 60 days before trial. Each side may serve no more than 10 interrogatories, 10 requests for production, and 10 requests for admission (each counting discrete subparts; document-genuineness requests excepted) on any other side, may take one deposition of each party (one representative for an entity) and up to 2 nonparty depositions, and may have 1 retained expert.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-74","t":"Motions in expedited civil actions","s":"Any 15-6-12(b) motion may be filed, but unless the court stays the case it does not postpone pleading or disclosure duties. Each party may file only one summary-judgment motion (which may raise several grounds), no later than 90 days before trial.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-75","t":"Procedure for expedited trials","s":"An expedited action is tried to the court unless a party serves a jury demand under 15-6-38(b); trial is set for a firm date and, absent good cause, must be held within 1 year of filing, and the parties must jointly file one set of proposed jury instructions and verdict forms (noting objections and alternatives) in word-processing format. A jury is 12 persons chosen from a panel of 18 with 3 strikes per side unless otherwise ordered or stipulated; a bench court may skip findings and decide on verdict forms or interrogatories with instructions, and may enter new verdicts instead of ordering a new trial. Trials should ordinarily go to the jury within 2 business days, with each side limited to 6 hours (objections, bench conferences, and for-cause challenges excluded). Documents noticed with copies at least 90 days before trial may be admitted over authenticity and hearsay objections if they qualify under listed exceptions in 19-19-803, objections are due within 30 days after notice, and health care provider statements under 19-19-803.2 are allowed.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-76","t":"Settlement conference and ADR in expedited actions","s":"Unless the parties agree or a contract or statute requires it, the court may not by order or local rule require a settlement conference or other ADR in an expedited civil action.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-76.1","t":"Preclusive effect of expedited judgments","s":"A judgment or order in an expedited civil action supports claim or issue preclusion only for a party, or one in privity with a party, to that action.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-77","t":"Courts of record and clerks","s":"Trial courts of record are always open for filing papers, issuing and returning process, and interlocutory motions and orders. Merits trials must be in open court; other proceedings may be held in chambers anywhere, but no non-ex parte hearing may be held outside the circuit without all affected parties’ consent. The clerk may grant routine process applications as of course, subject to the court’s review. Sections 15-6-78 and 15-6-79 are reserved.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-80","t":"Stenographic transcript as evidence","s":"When stenographically reported testimony from an earlier trial or hearing is admissible, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-81","t":"Applicability","s":"The chapter does not govern the statutory and other proceedings listed in Appendix A (for example plat vacation, election contests, forcible entry and detainer, habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, certiorari, contempt, eminent domain, partition, mortgage foreclosure by action, divorce, mechanics liens, and certain corporate dissolutions) to the extent their procedure is inconsistent with this chapter. Subdivision (b) is omitted, statutes on appeals to circuit court are not superseded, and any statute requiring an act ‘in the manner provided by law’ is performed under this chapter.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The chapter does not extend or limit circuit court jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-83","t":"Circuit court rules","s":"A majority of a circuit’s judges may adopt practice rules consistent with this chapter after notice filed with each county clerk in the circuit and a public hearing at least 30 days after notice (notice may be posted on the UJS or State Bar website, with email to State Bar members). Rules take effect only when filed with and approved by the Supreme Court and are published as an appendix to Title 15; Appendix B includes, for example, Sixth Circuit page limits for briefs and a Seventh Circuit pretrial checklist due 2 business days before the first pretrial conference.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-84","t":"Forms","s":"The Appendix of Forms (Appendix C) is sufficient under the chapter and illustrates the intended brevity; the sample summons gives 30 days after service to answer.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-85","t":"Title","s":"The chapter is known as the Rules of Civil Procedure and cited as RCP.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"},{"n":"15-6-86","t":"Effective date","s":"The chapter governs actions brought after July 1, 1966, and further proceedings in actions then pending unless applying it would be infeasible or unjust, in which case the prior procedure applies.","u":"https://sdlegislature.gov/Statutes/15-6"}]});
